selenium-webdriver-at-spi.py: build the XPath tree with pipelined D-Bus calls

Every XPath lookup (find element(s), page source) serialises the session
application's whole AT-SPI tree to XML. _createNode2 reads each node through
libatspi with about eight synchronous D-Bus round trips (role, role name,
name, description, accessible id, states, child count, child at index), so a
lookup costs ~1 ms per node: ~12 ms for the dock, 150-380 ms while krema's
Settings window (180-380 nodes) is open. It was half of the suite's time.

Qt's org.a11y.atspi.Cache.GetItems always returns an empty list, so no
single call returns the tree. _createTree sends each node's calls (GetRole,
the Name, Description and AccessibleId properties, GetState, GetChildren)
asynchronously on its own connection to the accessibility bus and requests a
node's children as soon as its GetChildren reply arrives, so all calls are in
flight at once and only the toolkit's own processing time remains. The XML
is the same as _createNode2's: role names from atspi_role_get_name(), state
names through pyatspi, AccessibleId omitted when the toolkit has none,
children in index order with null children skipped. Anything it cannot
reproduce exactly (a toolkit other than Qt, the desktop root, an error reply,
a child on another bus, a role outside libatspi's table) falls back to
_createNode2 for the whole tree.

A find element(s) lookup builds the tree only with the attributes its XPath
can read. The role (the tag name), the path and the children are always
there; the name, description, accessibility-id and states are read only
when the XPath names them after @ or attribute:: (_xpathAttributes). Any
other attribute reference (@*, @node(), an @ inside a string literal) reads
all of them. An XPath cannot observe an attribute it does not reference, so
it matches the same elements; a lookup like //tool_bar[@name='Krema Dock']
makes three calls per node instead of six. Python's handling of each reply
is about half of the build time, so the build takes about half as long.
The page source is always built with every attribute.

The locator walks each match's index path through libatspi and drops the
whole result when the path no longer leads to the matched object (the tree
is outdated). Upstream compares the name and description of the tree
element and the object it reached, so a lookup found nothing when the same
object changed its description in between: a dock item's description
("Active", "2 windows", "Starting") changes as windows open and activate.
For the pipelined build, which knows every element's D-Bus object path,
_liveObject checks the object path at each step instead and, where a
sibling added or removed before it shifted an index, finds the child among
the parent's current children by object path: a match is dropped only when
its object has left the tree. The synchronous build keeps the name and
description check.

